<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">History of writing</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The history of writing traces how writing systems developed and how their use transformed societies. Each invention of writing, the first within the last 6,000 years, grew out of proto-writing, symbols that could not fully record spoken language. Four independent inventions are most commonly recognised: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> about 3400–3100 BCE, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> about 3250 BCE, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> before about 1250 BCE and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> before about 1 CE. The first alphabetic writing was developed by workers in the Sinai Peninsula about 1800 BCE, and its descendant, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a>, was adapted into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabets</a>, from which most writing systems of Afro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> descend.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Writing systems typically satisfy three criteria: the writing has a purpose or meaning, it uses specific symbols recorded on some medium, and the symbols generally correspond to elements of spoken language. Where writing was invented independently, it emerged from proto-writing, which did not record language directly. Examples include the Jiahu symbols carved on tortoise shells in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the 7th millennium BCE, which most archaeologists do not link to true writing, and the Vinča symbols of the 6th to 5th millennia BCE.</p><p>Before the 20th century most scholars held that writing was invented once, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, and spread by diffusion. Knowledge of Mesoamerican systems established that it had been invented independently several times. Denise Schmandt-Besserat holds that writing began in the counting and cataloguing of agricultural produce.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform</a> emerged in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the late 4th millennium BCE as a means of keeping accounts, first as a numeral system pressed into clay with a round stylus. By the 29th century BCE writing with a wedge-shaped stylus included phonetic elements representing syllables of Sumerian. Geoffrey Sampson holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a> were probably invented under Sumerian influence, but later scholars find the evidence sparse, and glyphs found at Abydos in tomb U-J, dated between 3400 and 3200 BCE, point to independent development in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>The earliest attested Chinese characters are oracle bone and bronze inscriptions of the Late Shang period. The earliest Maya inscriptions date to the 3rd century BCE and the earliest that can be read to 199 CE. About 800 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> adapted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a> and introduced letters for vowels. The Etruscan civilization adapted a Western Greek alphabet, the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> borrowed from it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> letterforms reached their modern shapes by about 100 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The first codes of law were written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> about 2100 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> was inscribed on stone stelae. In Mesopotamia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> scribes also became stewards of astronomy, calendars and divination. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> made philological knowledge a qualification for officials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> argued that writing was an inferior means of transmitting learning, yet his dialogues survive as written works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>. Walter J. Ong, Jack Goody and Eric A. Havelock were among those who argued that literacy changed thought: Havelock attributed Greek philosophy to the written word, which allowed beliefs to be compared and examined.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “History of writing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
